n: LongTerm. Status: Active. Note: isClientStated = false.\n\nThe report also states: Client age 55, intended retirement age 60, self-employed earnings \"around £45,000 gross\", state pension payable from 2036-03-24 (age 67), full state pension £230.21 weekly. Emergency fund held £17,000 (confirmed sufficient). Foreseeable changes include: employment status change (2026-03-24), Nest plan becomes paid up (2026-03-24), inheritance receipt (2027-03-24, £350,000), retirement/reduced hours (2031-03-24), mortgage completion (2033-03-24, £750/month savings). Liquidity needs: sofa purchase £2,500 immediate (funded from cash), pension fund access from age 60 (funded from drawdown). Affordability: initial commitment £9,838.36, ongoing £3,181.08 annually, assessed as affordable based on pension funds plus inheritance being sufficient for retirement expenditure even after market fall, plus option to work beyond 60 or part-time.","fileSays":"The evidence supports most stated objectives and circumstances:\n\nMeeting summaries (P2, P5, P6, P7, P9, P10) confirm: Client is Melanie Anness, age 55, recently divorced, background in accounting and currency trading, self-employed with same company, two adult daughters (aged 30 and 29, financially independent), partner Tony (separate finances), type 2 diabetic (managed), updating will, setting up power of attorney with daughters. Seeking advice due to divorce, pension transfer, and financial planning post-divorce.\n\nP2 states: \"Your career as an accountant at Bridgeport Interiors Limited, where you earn an annual salary of £42,000\" — this contradicts the report's \"around £45,000 gross\" self-employed earnings.\n\nP9 states: \"At 55, Melanie is considering accessing her pension benefits to support herself financially during a transitional phase, with plans to return to work potentially in January\" and \"Melanie's essential monthly expenses are estimated at £1,700 to £1,800\" and \"She desires a monthly pension income of £1,900 to replace her lost employment income\" and \"Morgan confirmed that Melanie's overall monthly expenses, including her partner's contribution, amounted to £2,600.\" This describes a transitional phase with pension access, not the retirement at 60 scenario.\n\nP7 states: \"Your objectives include consolidating your various pension pots for better management and exploring tax-efficient strategies to optimise your retirement income. You are particularly interested in understanding the options available to you, such as the flexi-access drawdown and annuities, to ensure your retirement plans are both flexible and sustainable.\"\n\nP2 confirms: \"You are expecting to receive an inheritance of £350,000 from the sale of your late mother's property which is currently pending legal proceedings\" — matches OBJ6.\n\nP1 confirms: Risk profile 7 (Growth), capacity for loss moderate, sufficient funds for retirement with inheritance, option to work beyond 60 or part-time.\n\nP13 confirms: Investment preferences for medium-risk (risk level 7), diversified portfolio, approximately 80% growth assets and 20% defensive assets.\n\nP2 confirms: \"You are also in the process of updating your will and setting up power of attorney, appointing your daughters as attorneys\" — matches vulnerability adjustments.\n\nEvidence does NOT explicitly state: Priority ranking of objectives (1, 2, 3 pattern across objectives), specific target date of 2026-03-24 for Pension Sharing Order receipt, specific target date of 2031-03-24 for retirement at 60, specific annual retirement income target of £27,500, or that OBJ7 (IHT reduction) is adviser-identified rather than client-stated.","discrepancies":"[1] Income amount: Report states \"around £45,000 gross\" self-employed earnings; P2 states \"annual salary of £42,000\" as accountant at Bridgeport Interiors Limited. [2] Employment status and timing: Report states \"Change from employed to self-employed status\" as foreseeable change on 2026-03-24; P2 describes her as