] Part-time work post-retirement: Report mentions in capacity for loss assessment \"option to work beyond age 60 or to continue to work part time if necessary\"; Meeting Summary [P20] states client is \"considering part-time work in a different field, such as a garden centre, post-retirement\". The report treats part-time work as a contingency rather than a stated objective or plan. [9] Pension Sharing Order value: Report states \"approximately £260,000\"; Meeting Summary [P20] does not specify the PSO value. The extraction notes this is estimated/approximate (\"c. £260,000\"), but the report does not disclose the uncertainty or basis of this estimate. [10] Retirement age and access: Report recommends retirement at age 60 (2031-03-24); client can access pension from age 55 (current age). The 5-year gap between current age and planned retirement is not explained as a constraint or choice. [11] Linked objectives for recommendations: Report links REC-001 to OBJ1 only; REC-002 and REC-003 to OBJ2 and OBJ3; REC-004 and REC-005 to OBJ3 and OBJ4; REC-006 to OBJ1. However, the recommendations are not individually named or described in the extracted data—only the linked objective IDs are provided. The specific recommendation details (e.g., which fund, which provider action) are not matched to each objective in the report text provided.","comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Specific details of each recommendation (REC-001 through REC-006) beyond objective linkage","Reconciliation of £42,000 employed income vs £45,000 self-employed income","Basis and certainty of £350,000 inheritance amount","Details of personal loan and credit card liabilities and their repayment terms","Explanation of why retirement age 60 is chosen when access is available from age 55","Specific fund names and allocations for the recommended Aviva portfolio"],"analysis":"The report asserts that recommendations are linked to stated objectives, but the comparison reveals material gaps and inconsistencies between the report's assertions and the evidence in the case file. \n\n**Inheritance timing and amount**: The report assumes £350,000 inheritance receipt in 2027 as a certain cashflow input. However, the Meeting Summary [P20] explicitly states the inheritance timing is \"uncertain due to ongoing legal proceedings\" and the amount is \"at least £150,000\" from a property valued \"around £1.4 million\", not the £350,000 stated in the report. The report's cashflow modelling relies on this inheritance to demonstrate affordability for retirement, but the evidence shows both timing and amount are contingent. This is a material assumption not supported by the evidence.\n\n**Tax-free cash withdrawal**: The report infers a 25% tax-free lump sum withdrawal at outset (standard pension rule) but does not disclose the client's stated need for £40,000 tax-free cash with specific purposes: £20,000 for emergency funds and legal fees, £20,000 to pay off personal loan and credit card liabilities. The Fact Find [P18] shows these liabilities exist and are material to the affordability assessment (reducing monthly outgoings by £480), yet the report's financial position section does not list them. This is a missing component—the report does not address the client's specific access need for £40,000 or the liabilities that drive it.\n\n**Emergency fund and liquidity**: The report states the emergency fund of £17,000 is \"confirmed sufficient\" based on the client being \"comfortable with the concept as you have liquid cash should you require instance access funds\" [P2]. However, the Fact Find [P18] shows the client \"prefers to have 12 months of expenditure as an emergency reserve so close to £24,000\" and the current £7,000 \"is making her feel uncomfortable\". The report does not reconcile this preference with the stated sufficiency, nor does it explain how the £40,000 tax-free cash withdrawal will address the client's stated discomfort with emergency reserves.\n\n**Monthly income target**: The report plans regular withdrawals of £2,083.33 net/month (£25,000 annually), which falls within the stated range of £25,000-£30,000. However, the Fact Find [P18] specifies the client wants to \"replace with income from the pension of £1,900 per month\" (£22,800 annually), which is below the stated range. The report does not explain why the recommendation exceeds the client's stated replacement income target or whether this is affordable given the client's stated preference.\n\n**Employment income and pension contributions**: The report states \"around £45,000 gross\" self-employed earnings but does not reconcile this with the Meeting Summary [P20] which states the client earned \"£42,000 as an accountant at Bridgeport Interiors Limited\" before becoming self-employed. The report notes the client \"will no longer be entitled to employee benefits such as pension contributions\" but does not explain the impact on the affordability of the recommended strategy or the client's ability to make regular contributions to the new pension.\n\n**Foreseeable changes and access needs**: The report identifies foreseeable changes (inheritance, retirement at 60, mortgage completion) and access needs (sofa purchase, pension income from age 60), but does not explicitly link each recommendation to how it addresses these changes or needs. The recommendations are linked to objectives by ID only, without narrative explanation of how each recommendation serves each objective given the specific access needs and term.\n\n**Vulnerability overlay**: The report identifies the client as having \"Possible\" vulnerability due to recent divorce, employment change, and pending inheritance [P5]. The Fact Find [P18] shows the client is currently \"living off savings which is now reduced to £7,000\" and is \"making her feel uncomfortable\", and intends to use tax-free cash to cover legal fees for the inheritance proceedings. The report does not explain how the recommended strategy addresses the client's vulnerability or support needs (e.g., the stated intention to appoint daughters as Power of Attorney). The recommendation to consolidate pensions and set up drawdown is appropriate for the stated objectives, but the report does not address whether the timing and structure of the recommendation account for the client's current financial stress and vulnerability.\n\n**Recommendation linkage**: The report links recommendations to objectives by ID (e.g., REC-001 to OBJ1) but does not provide narrative explanation of how each recommendation specifically serves each objective. The extracted data does not include the full text of each recommendation, making it impossible to verify whether the linkage is specific or boilerplate. The guard against boilerplate linkage cannot be fully assessed without the full recommendation text.\n\n**Access mechanism**: The recommended Aviva Personal Pension allows access from age 55 (current minimum pension age) via flexi-access drawdown [P1], [P4]. The client is age 55 and plans to retire at age 60. The report does not explain why the client cannot access the pension immediately if needed, or whether the 5-year gap to planned retirement is a constraint or a choice. The access mechanism is appropriate for the stated objectives (flexible income from age 60), but the report does not address the client's immediate access needs (£40,000 tax-free cash for liabilities and emergency funds).\n\n**Discrepancies explained by guards**: The difference between the report's £2,083.33 monthly income and the Fact Find's £1,900 monthly target is not explained by a guard—it is a material difference in the stated income need. The report's assumption of £350,000 inheritance is not explained by a guard—it contradicts the evidence of uncertainty. The report's treatment of the emergency fund as \"sufficient\" is not explained by a guard—it contradicts the client's stated preference for £24,000 and discomfort with £7,000.\n\n**Conclusion on comparison**: The comparison can be performed. The report asserts that recommendations are appropriate for stated goals given access needs, term and foreseeable changes. However, the evidence shows: (1) material assumptions (inheritance amount and timing) are not supported and are contradicted by the case file; (2) specific access needs (£40,000 tax-free cash, personal loan/CC payoff) are not disclosed in the report; (3) the client's stated emergency fund preference (£24,000) is not addressed; (4) the client's stated monthly income target (£1,900) is not reconciled with the recommended withdrawal (£2,083.33); (5) the recommendations are linked to objectives by ID only, without narrative explanation of how each recommendation serves each objective given the specific access needs and term. These are material clarifications and additional evidence required to support the assertion that recommendations are appropriate.","citations":[{"passageId":"P20","source":"Meeting Summary with Jack.md","category":"C","quote":"You plan to retire at the age of 60. You aim to draw an annual pension of £25,000 to £30,000 until your state pension begins at 67. You are considering part-time work in a different field, such as a garden centre, post-retirement. You prefer a drawdown approach to receive a monthly income. You do not plan to take the 25% tax-free lump sum immediately.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P20","source":"Meeting Summary with Jack.md","category":"C","quote":"You anticipate a significant inheritance from your late mother's property, which is valued at around £1.4 million. You expect to receive at least £150,000 as your share, although the timeline for this inheritance is uncertain due to ongoing legal proceedings involving your mother's ex-partner.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P18","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":"She has stopped working and therefore lost employment income, she has stated that she would lie to replace with income from the pension of £1,900 per month. With the additional partner contribution of £800 towards bills this will put her very close to overall expenditure for the month with a slight reduction in luxuries, allowing her to live comfortably.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P18","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":"She is also wanting to withdraw a tax free cash lump sum of £40,000. £20,000 to replace emergency funds and also to cover some legal fees for the ongoing issue she has regarding the inheritance. The other £20,000 is to be used to pay off the personal loan and CC liabilities. Following the settle of these liabilities, she will greatly reduce her monthly outgoings by circa £480.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P18","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":"Currently living off savings which is now reduced to £7,000 and this also acts as an emergency pot. She prefers to have 12 months of expenditure as an emergency reserve so close to £24,000. The £7,000 will likely be down to circa £4,000 by the time the pension income is set up and this is making her feel uncomfortable.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"You are currently aged 55 and recently divorced, hence the Pension Sharing Order. You live with your partner Tony but maintain separate financial arrangements. You have two adult daughters who are in integral part of your estate planning, and you intend to appoint them as your Power of attorney. You have recently altered your employment status, going from employed to self-employed with the same company. You expect annual earnings to be around £45,000 gross going forward.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P20","source":"Meeting Summary with Jack.md","category":"C","quote":"You earn an annual salary of £42,000 as an accountant at Bridgeport Interiors Limited, which translates to a take-home pay of approximately £2,600 per month after pension and tax deductions.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"You understand the pension freedoms and prefer the flexibility of entering Flexi Access Drawdown when you access your pension, allowing you control over how and when benefits are taken.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Aviva Pension T&C.md","category":"H","quote":"You can normally choose via Your Adviser to take retirement Benefits, in the form of a Pension Commencement Lump Sum and/or by using Income Drawdown, from all or part of Your Pension Portfolio at any time from the Minimum Pension Age. This is currently age 55. From 6 April 2028 this will be age 57 unless You have a protected pension age.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Aviva Pension KFD.md","category":"H","quote":"You can normally choose to take benefits from your Pension Portfolio at any time from the normal minimum pension age. This is currently age 55. From 6 April 2028 this will be age 57 unless you have a protected pension age.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"You earn £45,000 per annum until age 60 at which point you retire. Your expenditure is £2,237 per month until retirement. You spend £2,500 on a new sofa immediately. You pay £750 per month on your mortgage for the next 7 years. You receive an inheritance of £350,000 next year. In retirement, your personal expenditure is £2,000 net each month.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"Based on your circumstances alone, your pension funds along with the inheritance will be sufficient to cover expenditure in retirement, even following a fall in the market. Additionally you have the option to work beyond age 60 or to continue to work part time if necessary.","cells":null}],"issueCategories":["Missing evidence","Weak justification","Missing component","Data inconsistency"],"severity":"Moderate","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

