[P1] [B] Fact Find (1).md (score 0.340)
- She has stopped working and therefore lost employment income, she has stated that she would lie to replace with income from the pension of £1,900 per month. With the additional partner contribution of £800 towards bills this will put her very close to overall expenditure for the month with a slight reduction in luxuries, allowing her to live comfortably.
- She is also wanting to withdraw a tax free cash lump sum of £40,000. £20,000 to replace emergency funds and also to cover some legal fees for the ongoing issue she has regarding the inheritance. The other £20,000 is to be used to pay off the personal loan and CC liabilities. Following the settle of these liabilities, she will greatly reduce her monthly outgoings by circa £480. Creating more disposable income.
- Currently living off savings which is now reduced to £7,000 and this also acts as an emergency pot. She prefers to have 12 months of expenditure as an emergency reserve so close to £24,000. The £7,000 will likely be down to circa £4,000 by the time the pension income is set up and this is making her feel uncomfortable.
- When she looks for another part time role at the start of 2027, she estimates the income from this to be minimum £20,000 per annum, but time will tell. The monthly income from the pension to be recommended will be assessed each month from affordability and tailored moving forward.
- **Cash reserves will be replenished through TFC and barrister fees covered for the foreseeable future.:** [blank]

[P2] [C] Meeting Summary with Jack.md (score 0.332)
## Objectives
You plan to retire at the age of 60.
You aim to draw an annual pension of £25,000 to £30,000 until your state pension begins at 67.
You are considering part-time work in a different field, such as a garden centre, post-retirement.
You prefer a drawdown approach to receive a monthly income.
You do not plan to take the 25% tax-free lump sum immediately.
## Income
You earn an annual salary of £42,000 as an accountant at Bridgeport Interiors Limited, which translates to a take-home pay of approximately £2,600 per month after pension and tax deductions. Your role as an accountant provides you with a stable income, which is crucial for managing your current expenses and planning for your future retirement. Despite your financial acumen, you recognise the importance of professional advice to optimise your retirement strategy, particularly in light of your recent divorce and the associated pension sharing order.
In addition to your salary, you anticipate a significant inheritance from your late mother's property, which is valued at around £1.4 million. You expect to receive at least £150,000 as your share, although the timeline for this inheritance is uncertain due to ongoing legal proceedings involving your mother's ex-partner. This potential windfall could play a pivotal role in your financial planning, offering an opportunity to bolster your retirement savings or address other financial goals, such as paying off your mortgage.
Your financial situation is further supported by your partner Tony's contribution to household expenses, which amounts to £600 a month. Although you maintain separate finances, this contribution helps alleviate some of the financial pressure, allowing you to focus on your long-term financial objectives. Your current income, combined with the anticipated inheritance and Tony's support, provides a solid foundation for your retirement planning, enabling you to explore various investment and pension strategies with the guidance of us.

