s and Conditions covering: crystallisation mechanics, benefit crystallisation events, drawdown options (single, phased, capped, flexi-access), structured products with fixed terms and potential penalties, commercial property restrictions, unit pricing mechanisms including smoothed pricing for managed funds, limit orders for ETIs, and complex withdrawal rules. [P9] (the report itself) describes personal pension plans as \"not subject to restrictions on charges and therefore tend to offer a wider range of investment opportunities and options than stakeholder pensions\" and explains flexi-access drawdown as providing \"a flexible and tax-efficient means of drawing benefits\" with \"no upper limit to the amount the individual can withdraw as an income\" but with each withdrawal \"taxed at their marginal rate of Income Tax.\" The report notes pensions will be \"included in your taxable estate for IHT\" from 6 April 2027. [P14] (report) states the client \"understand[s] the pension freedoms and prefer[s] the flexibility of entering Flexi Access Drawdown\" and notes death benefits are \"paid free of tax\" before age 75 but \"tax is paid at the beneficiary's marginal rate of tax\" after age 75. [P15] (report) describes the recommended funds as providing \"exposure to a range of asset classes and market sectors resulting in greater diversification and reduced risk\" and notes they are \"part of our CIP (Centralised Investment Proposition)\" with \"quarterly investment committee\" review. The fund literature [P22], [P24] shows the recommended funds are rated category 4-5 on risk scales, with material risks including counterparty risk, credit risk, default risk, derivatives risk, and emerging market risk, with asset allocations of 60%-90% equities and 10%-40% bonds.","discrepancies":"1. The report classifies the product as \"NonComplex\" but the product literature reveals multiple complex features: structured products with fixed terms and potential early exit penalties [P4], c