the operation of smoothed pricing, fund price adjustments, and dilution levies; (6) the use of derivatives in the recommended funds and associated risks; (7) the distinction between single drawdown, phased drawdown, and capped drawdown options and when each is appropriate.","analysis":"The report asserts the recommended product is \"NonComplex\" based on it being a \"standard personal pension on a regulated platform with access to standard investment funds\" with \"no exotic features, guarantees, or complex derivatives.\" However, the product literature reveals the Aviva Pension Platform offers substantially more complexity than this characterisation suggests. The Terms and Conditions [P2]-[P7], [P10]-[P13], [P20] describe: (1) multiple drawdown options (single, phased, capped, flexi-access) each with different rules and review requirements; (2) crystallisation mechanics requiring understanding of benefit crystallisation events; (3) structured products with fixed terms, minimum trade values, and potential penalties for early withdrawal; (4) commercial property and ETI investments requiring active selection; (5) complex unit pricing including smoothed prices and fund price adjustments; (6) limit orders with 30-day validity periods. The product literature [P22], [P24] explicitly identifies derivatives as a material risk and describes the recommended funds as having 60%-90% equity exposure with material risks including counterparty, credit, default, and derivatives risks. The report's statement that the client wants to \"keep the investment strategy fairly simple\" is inconsistent with recommending a platform offering such breadth of complex options. The report provides no evidence that the client understands or has been explained: the tax treatment of drawdown income at marginal rates; the IHT inclusion of pensions from 6 April 2027; the mechanics of crystallisation; structured product penalties; smoothed pricing; or derivatives use. The client's stated knowledge level is \"Moderate\" based on accounting and currency trading background, but no evidence demonstrates comprehension of pension-specific complexities such as crystallisation, drawdown mechanics, or IHT treatment. The report cites the client \"regularly check[s] the value of your funds\" and has \"a fair degree of knowledge and understanding in relation to pension planning\" but these statements do not evidence understanding of the specific complex features of the recommended product. The guard states \"A mainstream personal pension in multi-asset funds is not complex; do not escalate on product volume or value alone.\" The recommended product is indeed a mainstream personal pension in multi-asset funds, and the complexity drivers identified in the product literature (derivatives, structured products, complex drawdown mechanics, crystallisation rules) are features the client may not elect to use. However, the report's assertion that the product is \"NonComplex\" with \"no exotic features, guarantees, or complex derivatives\" is contradicted by the product literature, which explicitly describes derivatives as a material risk and structured products as available options. The report does not explain why these features are present in the product if they are not relevant to the client's needs, nor does it evidence that the client understands them or has been advised to avoid them. The vulnerability overlay notes the client has experienced recent divorce, employment change, and is expecting inheritance pending legal proceedings. These life events may affect the client's capacity to absorb complex product features or to make informed decisions about drawdown mechanics and IHT treatment. The report does not address how these vulnerabilities affect the suitability of a product with complex features.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"Aviva Pension KFD.md","category":"H","quote":"With the Self-Select phased income drawdown option, your adviser can create a mix of non-taxable and taxable income, tailored to your individual tax planning requirements.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Aviva Pension T&C.md","category":"H","quote":"Structured Products are fixed term investments, therefore there may be penalties payable if You leave the Structured Product before the end of the fixed term. If You wish to withdraw monies before the end of the fixed term, then You must notify Us, and the full Investment will need to be encashed. The amount You receive back will depend on the type of Structured Product You were invested in, and You may get back significantly less than the amount You initially invested.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Aviva Pension T&C.md","category":"H","quote":"Crystallisation occurs via Benefit Crystallisation Events. Each Drawdown Benefit Crystallisation Event will normally use the same arrangement within Your Pension Portfolio, unless We are instructed otherwise by Your Adviser or required by legislation to set up a separate arrangement.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"Aviva Pension T&C.md","category":"H","quote":"For the Smooth Managed Funds the valuations above will also be used to calculate the value of assets. When You buy and sell Units in the Smooth Managed Funds, You will receive a Smoothed Price which grows in line with a Smooth Growth Rate... Fund Price Adjustments will be applied to the Smoothed Price when the difference between the Smoothed Price and the Unsmoothed Price is greater than 6.5% and the Fund Price Adjustment will reduce the difference to 1.5%.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Aviva Pension T&C.md","category":"H","quote":"When dealing with ETIs, We may accept Limit Orders where You place limits on the price You are prepared to accept for a deal, provided that the order is above any minimum level We set from time to time. Any such order will be valid for a maximum of 30 days and defined by Your Adviser.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P20","source":"Aviva Pension T&C.md","category":"H","quote":"Choice is the investment option that gives You access to insured and collective Funds, Structured Products, Commercial Property, ETIs and Equities.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P22","source":"Vanguard& HSBC KIIDS.md","category":"H","quote":"Derivatives Risk Derivatives can behave unexpectedly. The pricing and volatility of many derivatives may diverge from strictly reflecting the pricing or volatility of their underlying reference(s), instrument or asset.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P24","source":"Vanguard & HSBC fact sheets.md","category":"H","quote":"The Fund may invest in different types of derivatives for efficient portfolio management purposes, including hedging, and for broader investment purposes to help the Fund meet its objective.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P9","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"Flexi-access drawdown provides a flexible and tax-efficient means of drawing benefits from a pension fund... There is no upper limit to the amount the individual can withdraw as an income. However, each withdrawal is taxed at their marginal rate of Income Tax.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P14","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"Pensions are currently outside of your estate for IHT purposes, however from 6 April 2027, most unused defined contribution pensions and death benefits (such as drawdown funds and uncrystallised pots) will be included in your taxable estate for IHT.","cells":null}],"issueCategories":["Weak justification","Missing evidence"],"severity":"Moderate","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

