- She has stopped working and therefore lost employment income, she has stated that she would lie to replace with income from the pension of £1,900 per month. With the additional partner contribution of £800 towards bills this will put her very close to overall expenditure for the month with a slight reduction in luxuries, allowing her to live comfortably.
- She is also wanting to withdraw a tax free cash lump sum of £40,000. £20,000 to replace emergency funds and also to cover some legal fees for the ongoing issue she has regarding the inheritance. The other £20,000 is to be used to pay off the personal loan and CC liabilities. Following the settle of these liabilities, she will greatly reduce her monthly outgoings by circa £480. Creating more disposable income.
- Currently living off savings which is now reduced to £7,000 and this also acts as an emergency pot. She prefers to have 12 months of expenditure as an emergency reserve so close to £24,000. The £7,000 will likely be down to circa £4,000 by the time the pension income is set up and this is making her feel uncomfortable.
- When she looks for another part time role at the start of 2027, she estimates the income from this to be minimum £20,000 per annum, but time will tell. The monthly income from the pension to be recommended will be assessed each month from affordability and tailored moving forward.
- **Cash reserves will be replenished through TFC and barrister fees covered for the foreseeable future.:** [blank]

