{"groupId":"G1.8","requirement":"Data mismatches and unsupported figures within the report itself — Consistency: Where client and family facts and financial information have been included in the Suitability Report, are they consistent throughout the suitability report (including facts used in all narrative sections, calculations, cashflow modelling, projections and assumptions) and with all supporting evidence files provided?","reportSays":"The suitability report asserts multiple financial and personal facts across narrative, tables, and calculations. Key figures include: client age 55, self-employed income £45,000 gross annually (described as \"around £45,000\"), mortgage balance £70,000 with £750/month repayment over 7 years, total assets £447,000 (main residence £430,000 + cash £17,000), net worth £377,000, three pension arrangements totalling £327,945.44 (Quilter c. £260,000 + True Potential £46,672.80 + Nest £21,272.64), Pension Sharing Order value c. £260,000 (approximate), inheritance £350,000 expected next year, state pension £230.21 weekly from age 67, retirement income target £25,000-£30,000 annually at age 60, agreed risk profile 7 (Growth), initial advice fee 3% of amount invested = £9,838.36, ongoing annual adviser charge 1% per annum = £3,181.08. The extraction report identifies six internal inconsistencies: (1) True Potential platform charge stated as 0.40% in existing arrangements table but 0.40% platform + 0.50% advice + 0.58% fund charges = 1.48% total in narrative; (2) Nest risk level 8 in table contradicts agreed risk level 7; (3) Quilter charge savings £208 per annum based on 0.08% difference on approximate fund value c. £260,000; (4) Initial fee £9,838.36 calculated as 3% of £327,945.44 but Pension Sharing Order value is approximate; (5) Risk profile 7 described as \"balanced\" relative to Nest level 8, creating ambiguity; (6) IHT analysis states current net assets £377,000 below £500,000 threshold, with inheritance taking total to £727,000 above threshold, but does not quantify pension inclusion from 6 April 2027.","fileSays":"This requirement compares the report against itself. No external evidence file is searched or required. The extraction report itself documents the internal inconsistencies found within the canonical model.","discrepancies":"[1] True Potential platform charge: stated as 0.40% in existing arrangements table but narrative section states 0.40% platform + 0.50% advice + 0.58% fund charges = 1.48% total. The table shows only platform charge without separating advice and fund charges. [2] Nest risk level: table shows risk level 8 but narrative states agreed risk level is 7 and \"The current fund is not in line with the agreed risk level\". [3] Quilter charge savings calculation: stated as £208 per annum based on 0.08% difference, which reconciles to £260,000 fund value (0.08% × £260,000 = £208), but the Pension Sharing Order value is stated as \"c. £260,000\" (approximate), introducing uncertainty into the savings figure. [4] Initial advice fee: stated as £9,838.36 calculated as 3% of total transfer value £327,945.44, but the Pension Sharing Order component (c. £260,000) is approximate, making the fee contingent on final values. [5] Risk profile characterisation: client's agreed risk profile 7 is described as \"balanced\" and \"less aggressive than\" Nest fund at level 8, but the True Potential portfolio shows risk level 6 in table (below agreed level 7), creating inconsistency in how misalignment is characterised. [6] IHT analysis: narrative states current net assets £377,000 are below £500,000 threshold and inheritance of £350,000 will take total to £727,000 above threshold, but does not quantify the impact of pension inclusion from 6 April 2027 (pension values c. £327,945 would add significantly to taxable estate). [7] Income description: self-employed earnings stated as \"around £45,000 gross\" in narrative but used as exact £45,000 in cashflow modelling; the word \"around\" suggests approximation. [8] Mortgage reconciliation: mortgage balance £70,000 with 7 years remaining at £750/month; total paid over 7 years would be £750 × 12 × 7 = £63,000, which does not reconcile with stated £70,000 balance, suggesting either the balance or term is approximate or includes additional costs.","comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report contains multiple internal inconsistencies that create material ambiguity about the accuracy and consistency of key figures used in the suitability analysis. \n\nDiscrepancy [1] — True Potential charges: The existing arrangements table presents platform charge as 0.40% in isolation, while the replacement analysis narrative breaks down the total charge as 0.40% platform + 0.50% advice + 0.58% fund charges = 1.48%. This is not a contradiction of the same figure but a difference in presentation scope: the table shows platform charge only, while the narrative shows the full charge stack. However, this creates ambiguity about what \"0.40%\" means in the table context and whether the table is incomplete or the narrative is adding components not previously disclosed. This is a presentation inconsistency that affects clarity of charge comparison.\n\nDiscrepancy [2] — Nest risk level: The existing arrangements table states Nest risk level as 8, but the client's agreed risk profile is 7 (Growth), and the narrative explicitly states \"The current fund is not in line with the agreed risk level\". This is a direct contradiction: the table asserts risk level 8, the narrative confirms the fund is misaligned with agreed level 7. The table value contradicts the client profile and the narrative assessment. This is a material inconsistency because risk alignment is central to suitability.\n\nDiscrepancy [3] — Quilter charge savings: The report states \"savings of £208 per annum\" based on 0.27% vs 0.19% (0.08% difference). On £260,000, this calculates correctly (0.08% × £260,000 = £208). However, the Pension Sharing Order value is stated as \"c. £260,000\" (circa/approximately), and the narrative notes this is \"based on the estimated fund value\". The savings figure is therefore contingent on a value that is explicitly approximate. The arithmetic is correct but the underlying assumption is uncertain. This creates a finding about the reliability of the savings claim, not an arithmetic error.\n\nDiscrepancy [4] — Initial advice fee: The report states £9,838.36 as 3% of total transfer value. The calculation is: 3% × (£67,945.44 + £260,000) = 3% × £327,945.44 = £9,838.36. This reconciles arithmetically. However, the Pension Sharing Order value is approximate (\"c. £260,000\"), and the narrative states \"this figure may fluctuate depending on transfer values\". The fee is therefore contingent on final values. The arithmetic is internally consistent but the underlying assumption is uncertain. This is not an inconsistency in the report's own figures but a disclosure that the fee is provisional.\n\nDiscrepancy [5] — Risk profile characterisation: The client's agreed risk profile is 7 (Growth). The recommended funds both have risk rating 7. The narrative states the recommended funds are \"more balanced and in line with your risk tolerance\" and have \"understandably lagged behind the higher-risk funds\" (implying they are less aggressive than Nest level 8). However, the True Potential portfolio shows risk level 6 in the table, which is below the agreed level 7. The narrative criticises True Potential as \"not in line with the agreed risk level\" of 7, which is consistent (6 < 7). But the characterisation of the recommended funds at level 7 as \"balanced\" relative to level 8 is somewhat ambiguous — it could mean they are balanced in absolute terms or balanced relative to the higher-risk Nest fund. This is not a direct contradiction but an ambiguity in how risk levels are characterised across the report.\n\nDiscrepancy [6] — IHT analysis: The narrative correctly states current net assets £377,000 are below the £500,000 threshold (NRB £325,000 + RNRB £175,000), and that inheritance of £350,000 will take the total to £727,000, exceeding the threshold. However, the report notes that \"From 6 April 2027, most unused defined contribution pensions and death benefits will be included in your taxable estate\" but does not quantify the impact. The pension values (Quilter c. £260,000 + True Potential £46,672.80 + Nest £21,272.64 = c. £327,945) would add significantly to the taxable estate from that date. The IHT summary states \"estimated IHT liability\" as £0 as at 2026-03-24, which is correct for the current position, but the narrative does not quantify the future IHT position after 6 April 2027 when pensions are included. This is not an inconsistency within the report's own figures but an incomplete analysis of a stated fact.\n\nDiscrepancy [7] — Income description: The report states \"around £45,000 gross\" in narrative but uses £45,000 as an exact figure in cashflow modelling. The word \"around\" suggests approximation, but the figure is treated as exact in calculations. This creates ambiguity about whether £45,000 is a precise figure or an estimate. The extraction report flags this as an ambiguity.\n\nDiscrepancy [8] — Mortgage reconciliation: The report states mortgage balance £70,000 with 7 years remaining at £750/month. The total paid over 7 years would be £750 × 12 × 7 = £63,000, which does not reconcile with the stated £70,000 balance. This suggests either: (a) the balance is approximate, (b) the 7-year term is approximate, or (c) there are additional costs (interest, fees) not stated. The extraction report flags this as an ambiguity. The discrepancy is £7,000 (£70,000 - £63,000), which is material (10% of the stated balance).\n\nOf these eight discrepancies, [2] is a direct contradiction (Nest risk level 8 vs agreed level 7). Discrepancies [3], [4], and [6] involve figures that are explicitly stated as approximate or contingent, which is a disclosure issue rather than an inconsistency per se, but the use of these approximate figures in precise calculations (e.g., £208 savings, £9,838.36 fee) creates ambiguity about reliability. Discrepancies [1], [5], [7], and [8] involve ambiguities or incomplete reconciliations flagged by the extraction report.\n\nThe most material inconsistency is [2]: the Nest fund is stated to have risk level 8 in the existing arrangements table, but the client's agreed risk profile is 7, and the narrative confirms the fund is \"not in line with the agreed risk level\". This is a direct contradiction within the report's own data.\n\nDiscrepancies [3], [4], and [6] are not inconsistencies in the strict sense but rather uses of approximate or incomplete figures in precise calculations or analyses. However, they represent a data quality issue: figures stated as approximate are used in calculations that imply precision, and incomplete analyses (e.g., IHT without pension inclusion) are presented without caveats.\n\nDiscrepancy [8] (mortgage reconciliation) is material: a £7,000 gap between stated balance and calculated total payments is not explained and suggests either the balance or the term is approximate or incorrect.\n\nDiscrepancy [7] (income \"around £45,000\") is a presentation issue: the word \"around\" suggests approximation, but the figure is used as exact in modelling.\n\nDiscrepancy [1] (True Potential charges) is a presentation inconsistency: the table shows platform charge only, while the narrative shows the full charge stack. This is not a contradiction of the same figure but a difference in scope that creates ambiguity.\n\nDiscrepancy [5] (risk profile characterisation) is an ambiguity in how risk levels are described, not a direct contradiction.\n\nThe requirement asks whether client and family facts and financial information are consistent throughout the report and with supporting evidence. This is a self-consistency check. The report contains at least one direct contradiction (Nest risk level) and several material ambiguities or incomplete reconciliations (mortgage, income, charges, IHT). These are findings on data consistency within the report itself.","citations":[{"passageId":"[I]","source":"Suitability report 2026.pdf — Existing Arrangements table","category":"I","quote":"Nest plan shows risk level 8 in the table","cells":null},{"passageId":"[I]","source":"Suitability report 2026.pdf — Your attitude to risk section","category":"I","quote":"Client's agreed risk profile is 7 (Growth)","cells":null},{"passageId":"[I]","source":"Suitability report 2026.pdf — narrative section","category":"I","quote":"The current fund is not in line with the agreed risk level","cells":null},{"passageId":"[I]","source":"Suitability report 2026.pdf — Existing Arrangements table","category":"I","quote":"True Potential platform charge stated as 0.40%","cells":null},{"passageId":"[I]","source":"Suitability report 2026.pdf — Replacement Analysis section","category":"I","quote":"Platform Charges 0.40% with ongoing advice charge 0.50% and weighted average fund management charges 0.58%, totalling 1.48%","cells":null},{"passageId":"[I]","source":"Suitability report 2026.pdf — Replacement Analysis section","category":"I","quote":"savings of £208 per annum based on 0.27% vs 0.19% (0.08% difference)","cells":null},{"passageId":"[I]","source":"Suitability report 2026.pdf — Pension Sharing Order section","category":"I","quote":"Pension Sharing Order value c. £260,000 (approximately)","cells":null},{"passageId":"[I]","source":"Suitability report 2026.pdf — Costs and Charges section","category":"I","quote":"Initial advice fee of 3% of the amount invested = £9,838.36","cells":null},{"passageId":"[I]","source":"Suitability report 2026.pdf — Income section","category":"I","quote":"Self-employed earnings stated as 'around £45,000 gross'","cells":null},{"passageId":"[I]","source":"Suitability report 2026.pdf — Liabilities section","category":"I","quote":"Mortgage balance £70,000 with 7 years remaining at £750/month","cells":null},{"passageId":"[I]","source":"Suitability report 2026.pdf — Inheritance Tax Summary","category":"I","quote":"Currently, your assets are below your allowance of £500,000 meaning you have no Inheritance Tax liability. However, on receipt of the inheritance this will take you over the limit.","cells":null},{"passageId":"[I]","source":"Suitability report 2026.pdf — Estate Planning section","category":"I","quote":"From 6 April 2027, most unused defined contribution pensions and death benefits will be included in your taxable estate","cells":null}],"issueCategories":["Data inconsistency","Data quality"],"severity":"Moderate","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

