ence on file. (1) Preference contradiction: The certified Pension Sharing Annex [P6] records the client's stated preference as \"Internal transfer\" (marked). The report asserts the opposite — \"You mentioned during our meeting that you had no ties or preference to leave the funds with Quilter\" — recommending external transfer to Aviva. This is a direct contradiction of a documented preference. (2) Quilter evidence absent: The report's rationale for switching Quilter rests on charge comparisons (0.27% vs 0.19% or 0.21%), but no Quilter evidence exists on file — no illustration, charges schedule, fund list, features, risk level or projection. The report's own table records \"Investment Funds: N/A\" and \"Risk Level: N/A\" for Quilter. £260,000 (79% of the money) is moved on an unsupported basis. (3) Switching analysis incomplete: The report presents switching analysis as the critical yield for the recommendation. However, Expected Results [P9] states the analysis \"excludes the £260,000 pension credit entirely. All four SelectaPension runs cover only True Potential and Nest (£67,945.44), and Quilter is absent from the Pension Switch Charges table.\" This means the largest component of the recommendation lacks any switching analysis. (4) Switching analysis conflicting: Four switching analyses were run with materially different outcomes: −12.42% (all charges inc. existing OAC), −20.75% (all charges ex. existing OAC), +8.97% (initial charge only), +12.34% (no charges). The report's body presents the two most favourable figures (per-plan sub-tables) and concludes \"overall lower costs\", while the unfavourable combined result (−12.42% or −20.75%) is confined to the appendix. This selective presentation obscures the true cost impact. (5) Flexible retirement rationale contradicted: The report states \"The receiving scheme provides more flexible retirement options\" as a reason to switch Quilter. However, the report's own table records Quilter as already offering \"Flexi-access drawdown, full and partial UFPLS\", and the report itself notes Nest offers no flexi-access drawdown. This rationale is internally contradicted. (6) Nest employment status unevidenced: The report's rationale for transferring Nest rests on \"You have recently altered your employment status, going from employed to self-employed with the same company. You will no longer be entitled to employee benefits such as pension contributions and your Nest plan will become paid up.\" The Fact Find [P5] notes \"Nest steps - Tax Free cash withdrawal of £40,000 to cover fees\" but does not confirm the employment status change or that Nest will become paid up. The meeting summaries [P1, P2, P3] do not discuss this change. (7) Alternatives absent: The report provides no discussion of alternatives considered or reasons for rejection. Expected Results [P9] notes \"Five options are listed and none is discussed. Option 1 (leave untouched) is the one the adviser's own analysis says is better by 12.42%\". The report does not explain why the option with the best charge outcome was rejected. (8) Vulnerability overlay: The client is recorded as having \"Possible\" vulnerability due to recent divorce, employment change, and pending inheritance [P3]. The report's rationale does not address how these life events affect the suitability of the recommendations or the timing of the switches. The divorce-related Pension Sharing Order is pending legal proceedings, and the employment change is recent. These circumstances materially affect the suitability assessment but are not reflected in the rationale. (9) Boilerplate rationale: Six rationale statements are marked isClientSpecific=false. The statement \"By moving to the recommended scheme, I will have the ability to actively monitor and adjust your investments as required, helping to ensure they remain aligned with your changing needs and market conditions\" appears verbatim twice (for REC-002 and REC-005). Generic statements such as \"The charg