ng monthly returns from March 2025 to February 2026; (3) switching analysis documents (SelectaPension runs) comparing True Potential and Nest to Aviva, but notably excluding Quilter entirely from the switching analysis; (4) the Nest Higher Risk fund documentation showing its asset class composition and investment approach; (5) an expected results document flagging that the switching analysis excludes the £260,000 Pension Sharing Order entirely, that four different switching analyses were run with materially different outcomes (ranging from −20.75% to +12.34%), and that the body reports only the two most favourable results; (6) the expected results also note that Quilter evidence is absent from the case file (no illustration, plan information form, charges schedule, fund list, feature comparison or projection), and that the single figure of 0.27% platform charges is unsupported. The file records that the Pension Sharing Order value is approximate ('c. £260,000') and that the inheritance timing is uncertain ('currently pending legal proceedings').","discrepancies":"[1] Quilter platform charge comparison: Report asserts 0.27% for Quilter vs 0.19% for Aviva (£208 saving), but no Quilter evidence exists in the case file to support the 0.27% figure. [2] Switching analysis scope: Report presents switching analysis as basis for recommendation, but the SelectaPension runs exclude Quilter (£260,000, 79% of total funds) entirely, covering only True Potential and Nest (£67,945.44). [3] Switching analysis outcomes: Four different SelectaPension runs show materially different results (−20.75% to +12.34%), but the report body cites only the most favourable figures (True Potential: 0.01% underperformance; Nest: 1.23% outperformance required) and concludes 'overall lower costs'. [4] True Potential charge comparison: Report states platform charges 0.21% (Aviva) vs 0.40% (True Potential), but the existing arrangements table in the report itself shows True Potential platform charge as 