ended charges as a percentage and in absolute terms. 4. Derivation and supporting calculation for the 1.23% required outperformance figure. 5. Transaction costs, exit fees, or transfer charges associated with the switches. 6. Quantification of the benefits claimed (professional management, transparent platform, consolidation) and how they offset the cost difference. 7. Confirmation of the final transfer values to verify the initial fee amount (currently contingent on approximate values).","analysis":"The requirement asks whether costs are excessive relative to benefits. The report asserts a required outperformance of 1.23% per annum but provides no supporting cost comparison or benefit analysis to justify this figure. The comparison cannot be performed because: (a) the report does not disclose the total ongoing charges for the existing arrangements in a clear, aggregated form; (b) the report does not disclose the total ongoing charges for the recommended arrangement in a clear, aggregated form; (c) the report does not show a side-by-side comparison of existing vs. recommended costs; (d) the report does not explain how the 1.23% figure was calculated or what assumptions underpin it; (e) transaction costs are explicitly absent from the report; (f) the benefits claimed (professional management, transparent platform) are stated qualitatively but not quantified or linked to the cost difference. The extraction notes internal inconsistency regarding True Potential charges (0.40% vs. 1.48% total), which undermines confidence in the baseline for comparison. The initial fee is contingent on approximate transfer values, introducing uncertainty. The report states the recommendation is affordable and provides benefits, but does not demonstrate that these benefits justify the 1.23% annual outperformance requirement or that costs are not excessive. The vulnerability overlay notes the client is recently divorced, recently changed employment status, and expecting a pending inheritance — all life events that may affect the client's capacity to absorb cost impacts, but the report does not address how costs interact with these vulnerabilities. The report's silence on aggregated costs, cost comparison, and benefit quantification means the claim that costs are justified cannot be assessed against evidence.","citations":[{"passageId":null,"source":"Suitability report 2026.pdf","category":"I_RecommendationsAndAdvice","quote":"your proposed plan will need to outperform your current plan by 1.23% per annum to age 89 in order to meet the same value as your current plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":null,"source":"Suitability report 2026.pdf","category":"I_RecommendationsAndAdvice","quote":"Initial advice fee of 3% of the amount invested, paid directly by the pension scheme on transfer; ongoing annual adviser charge of 1% per annum, paid via the Aviva Platform","cells":null},{"passageId":null,"source":"Suitability report 2026.pdf","category":"I_RecommendationsAndAdvice","quote":"the recommended funds, being more balanced and in line with your risk tolerance, has understandably lagged behind the higher-risk funds","cells":null},{"passageId":null,"source":"Extraction metadata","category":"I_RecommendationsAndAdvice","quote":null,"cells":["Transaction costs","NotPresentInDocument"]}],"issueCategories":["Missing evidence","Weak justification","Disclosure shortfall"],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

