et showing OCF 0.20%, which aligns with the stated 0.20% fund charge). The True Potential 0.40% platform charge is stated in the report but the internal inconsistency note flags ambiguity about whether this is platform-only or includes advice and fund charges. The £88.68 saving calculation is arithmetically correct on the True Potential fund value of £46,672.80. (2) Fund charge comparison: The report states existing fund charges are 0.58% and new plan charges are 0.20%. The 0.20% figure is supported by [P9] (Vanguard OCF 0.20%). However, the 0.58% figure for existing arrangements is not explicitly stated in the evidence. The True Potential holdings [P3, P12] show individual fund performance but do not state ongoing annual charges. The £177.35 saving calculation appears to be based on True Potential fund value alone (0.38% difference × £46,672.80 = £177.35), but this is not explicitly stated. If the comparison is meant to apply to all three arrangements, the basis is unclear. (3) Ongoing adviser charge: The report discloses \"ongoing annual adviser charge of 1% per annum\" in the fee agreement basis section, but this is not prominently featured in the main narrative or in a consolidated cost comparison. This is a material charge that should be clearly set against the stated benefits. The 1% ongoing charge on £327,945.44 total would be approximately £3,279 annually, which is substantial and not clearly quantified in the suitability narrative. (4) Initial fee: The £9,838.36 initial fee (3% of £327,945.44) is arithmetically sound, but the Pension Sharing Order value is approximate (\"c. £260,000\"), so the fee is contingent on final transfer values. This contingency is not clearly disclosed. (5) Cumulative cost impact: The report does not provide an aggregated all-in cost comparison. The stated savings (£88.68 platform + £177.35 fund = £266.03 annually) are modest relative to the initial fee of £9,838.36 and the ongoing adviser charge of approximately £3,279 annually. The payback period on the initial fee alone would be approximately 37 years (£9,838.36 ÷ £266.03), which is not addressed. (6) Benefit justification: The report states benefits including \"active monitoring and adjustment\", \"wider range of investment options\", \"flexible retirement options\", and \"tax-efficient withdrawal strategies\". These are generic benefits not clearly linked to the cost structure or quantified. The report does not explain why these benefits justify the 1% ongoing adviser charge or the £9,838.36 initial fee. (7) Performance comparison: The report cites existing fund performance (True Potential 38.53%, Nest 47.84% cumulative over 5 years) but does not clearly state whether these are annualised or cumulative. The derived figures note these should be annualised as 6.74% and 8.13% respectively. The recommended fund performance (59.27% cumulative to end February 2026) is shown in the SAP illustrations but the time period is not clearly aligned with the existing fund comparison period. The report states the recommended funds \"have performed well in comparison with their peers\" but does not provide a direct performance comparison with the existing funds over the same period. (8) Vulnerability overlay: The client is identified as having possible vulnerability due to recent divorce, employment change, and pending inheritance. The report does not address whether the 1% ongoing adviser charge is justified given the client's circumstances or whether the active monitoring benefit is material to the client's needs. The Fact Find [P20] notes \"Tax Free cash withdrawal of £40,000 to cover fees\" but does not clarify whether this is sufficient to cover the initial fee and ongoing charges, or whether the client understands the ongoing cost commitment.","citations":[{"passageId":"P9","source":"Vanguard & HSBC fact sheets.md","category":"H","quote":"Ongoing Charges Figure (OCF 2 ) 0.20%","cells":null},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Annes M SAP All charges ex exi