h a decrease in Annual Management Charge of 0.08%, but the full ongoing charge structure (including adviser charge and fund charges) is not clearly itemised in the main report narrative. The report discloses that \"Details of these funds are contained in the product literature\" ([P5]), delegating detailed fund risk information to external documents.","discrepancies":"1. The report states a -12.42% effect of switching in the critical yield table ([P2]), but this figure is presented only in tabular form within the analysis section, not explained in narrative prose in the main body or the dedicated \"Other Considerations of Switching\" section. 2. The report presents generic risk warnings (\"unit prices and the value of your pension fund can fall as well as rise\") but does not quantify or illustrate specific loss scenarios for the recommended Aviva solution; no worked example of a fall in fund value is provided. 3. The report notes that recommended funds have \"understandably lagged behind the higher-risk funds\" ([P4]) but does not explain the material disadvantage this represents in terms of projected retirement income or fund value at retirement. 4. The report discloses that \"Details of these funds are contained in the product literature\" ([P5]), delegating detailed product-specific risks to external documents rather than summarising material risks in the report itself. 5. The critical yield analysis shows the Aviva solution requires 0.41%–0.44% higher annual growth than existing schemes, but this is not explained in the \"Other Considerations of Switching\" section; it appears only in the technical analysis section. 6. The report does not explicitly state what guarantees or protections the client is giving up by switching from existing arrangements (e.g., any guarantees in the True Potential or Nest plans). 7. Tax consequences of the pension transfer and consolidation are not addressed in the disadvantages section; the report discusses IHT implications but not income tax or withdrawal tax consequences of the new arrangement.","comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":"1. No worked example or illustration of a loss scenario (e.g., fund value fall of 20% or 30%) and its impact on retirement income is provided in the report. 2. No explicit statement of what guarantees, protections or features the client is giving up by switching from existing arrangements. 3. No detailed breakdown of ongoing charges in the main report narrative; the full charge structure (platform charge, adviser charge, fund charges) is referenced in illustrations but not summarised in the report's disadvantages section. 4. No quantification of the impact of the -12.42% switching effect on projected retirement fund value or income.","analysis":"The report does present a dedicated section titled \"Other Considerations of Switching\" on page 9, satisfying the structural requirement for a dedicated section. However, the consistency between what the report asserts about disadvantages and what the supporting evidence shows reveals several material gaps and inconsistencies. \n\nFirst, the critical yield analysis in [P2] shows a -12.42% effect of switching, which is a material disadvantage. This figure appears in a table but is not explained in narrative form in the \"Other Considerations of Switching\" section. The table shows that if the Aviva solution experiences the same investment growth as the existing schemes, the fund value will be 12.42% lower due to the switching effect (charges and transition costs). This is a quantified, material disadvantage that should be prominently explained in the dedicated disadvantages section, but it is not. Instead, it appears only in the technical analysis section, making it less prominent than the benefits discussion.\n\nSecond, the report states generic risk warnings (\"unit prices and the value of your pension fund can fall as well as rise\") but provides no specific loss scenario illustration. The report does not show, for example, what would happen to the projected retirement fund value if markets fell by 20% or 30% in the early years. This is a material omission for a client with a 5-year investment horizon to retirement at age 60.\n\nThird, the report notes that the recommended funds have \"understandably lagged behind the higher-risk funds\" ([P4]), but does not explain the material disadvantage this represents. The performance table in [P8] shows the True Potential fund returned 38.53% cumulatively over 5 years, while the recommended Risk 7 HSBC/Vanguard portfolio returned 45.91%. However, the report does not quantify the impact of this underperformance on projected retirement income or fund value. The critical yield analysis in [P2] shows the Aviva solution requires 0.41%–0.44% higher annual growth to match existing schemes, but this is not explained in the disadvantages section.\n\nFourth, the report delegates detailed product-specific risks to external documents: \"Details of these funds are contained in the product literature\" ([P5]). This means material investment risks and product features are not summarised in the report itself, only referenced. While the report does include some generic risk warnings, product-specific risks (e.g., liquidity constraints, fund closure risk, manager changes) are not disclosed in the report.\n\nFifth, the report does not explicitly state what guarantees or protections the client is giving up by switching. The extraction notes indicate that \"featuresOrGuaranteesLost\" is absent from the replacement analysis, suggesting the report does not address this material disadvantage.\n\nSixth, tax consequences of the pension transfer are not addressed in the disadvantages section. The report discusses Inheritance Tax implications but does not explain income tax or withdrawal tax consequences of the new arrangement, such as the impact of the pension inclusion in the taxable estate from 6 April 2027.\n\nThe vulnerability overlay is relevant here. The