charge from every cost comparison. The \"Like for Like\" analysis compares True Potential's 0.98% with Aviva's 0.39%, but this excludes the adviser's 1% charge, making the true comparison 1.48% (True Potential with existing 0.50% advice) vs 1.39% (Aviva with new 1% advice) — a reduction of only 0.09%, not the 0.59% stated. For Nest, the comparison is 0.30% vs 1.39% (not 0.39%), a net increase of 1.09% or approximately £3,181 per annum on the total fund. The report states this £3,181 figure in the affordability section but never brings it into the cost comparison. The Quilter comparison (79% of the money) is entirely absent from the switching analysis, with no supporting documentation on file.\n\nOn projected outcomes: The report projects fund values to age 89, but the SelectaPension analyses run to age 87. More significantly, four switching analyses were run with materially different outcomes (−12.42%, −20.75%, +8.97%, +12.34%), and the body reports the two most favourable per-plan figures while the unfavourable combined result (−12.42%) appears only in the appendix. The report states \"can underperform… by 0.01%\" for True Potential, but this is the sub-table figure from the most favourable variant; the combined effect is −12.42%.\n\nOn cumulative charge impact: The Aviva illustration shows charges reducing the fund at age 60 from £372,000 to £337,000 — a reduction in yield of 2.2% — yet the report presents the total charge as 0.39% and states \"The charges are competitive.\" This cumulative effect is not explained to the client in the main narrative.\n\nOn loss scenarios: The report states disadvantages (market timing risk, access delay, capital risk) but does not quantify any loss scenario. No illustration shows the effect of a market fall on retirement income adequacy, the impact of the 10-15 day access delay on liquidity needs, or the consequence of capital loss in early years.\n\nThe guards permit weak justification to be graded rather than treated as absent, and they note that where costs fall and projections improve, absence of justification is not a finding. However, here costs do not clearly fall (the new adviser charge is omitted from comparisons), projections are presented selectively (the unfavourable combined result is in the appendix), and the cumulative effect of charges is not disclosed. The report's assertion that costs are \"lower\" and \"competitive\" is not supported by the evidence when the full adviser charge is included, and the selective presentation of switching analyses undermines the justification offered.\n\nVulnerability overlay: The client is recorded as having possible vulnerability due to recent divorce, employment change, and pending inheritance. The pending inheritance is material to the capacity-for-loss assessment (F3.1 notes it is overstated by £200,000 and contested in court). The report's cost justification does not address how the increased ongoing charge (1% vs the existing 0.50% on True Potential) affects the client's ability to meet retirement income needs, particularly given the uncertainty around the inheritance and the client's stated preference for flexible income from age 60. The vulnerability characteristics make the omission of the full adviser charge from cost comparisons more material, as the client may be less able to absorb the additional cost.","citations":[{"passageId":"P17","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"Based on information supplied by True Potential, excluding the current ongoing advice charges, our calculations show that the Aviva plan is 0.59% less expensive than your current True Potential plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P17","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"Based on information supplied by your existing provider, Nest our calculations show that the Aviva plan is 0.09% more expensive than your current Nest plan. This means that your proposed plan will need to outperform your current plan by £19.15 per annum.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P18","source":"expected-results.md","category":null,"quote":"Every cost comparison in the report omits the new 1% ongoing adviser charge. 'Like for Like' compares True Potential's 0.98% with Aviva's 0.39% and Nest's 0.30% with 0.39%. Including the new charge the client moves to 1.39% on the whole £327,945 — from 1.48% (True Potential, including its existing 0.50% advice charge), from 0.30% (Nest) and from 0.27% (Quilter). That is roughly £3,181 a year","cells":null},{"passageId":"P18","source":"expected-results.md","category":null,"quote":"The combined −12.42% reduction in projected fund value and the +0.43% critical yield uplift appear only in the appendix, only for one of four variants, while the body states the opposite conclusion","cells":null},{"passageId":"P18","source":"expected-results.md","category":null,"quote":"The cumulative effect of charges is never disclosed. The Aviva illustration shows all charges reducing the fund at age 60 from £372,000 to £337,000 — growth after inflation cut from 2.8% to 0.6%, a reduction in yield of 2.2%","cells":null},{"passageId":"P21","source":"expected-results.md","category":null,"quote":"The switching analysis excludes the £260,000 pension credit entirely. All four SelectaPension runs cover only True Potential and Nest (£67,945.44), and Quilter is absent from the Pension Switch Charges table.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P21","source":"expected-results.md","category":null,"quote":"Four switching analyses were run with materially different answers and the body reports the two most favourable. Combined effect on the fund: −12.42% (all charges inc. existing OAC), −20.75% (all charges ex. existing OAC), +8.97% (initial charge only), +12.34% (no charges). The body reports 'can underperform… by 0.01%' (the True Potential sub-table of the inc-OAC run) and 'will need to outperform… by 1.23%' (Nest), and concludes 'overall lower costs'","cells":null},{"passageId":"P20","source":"Suitability report