{"groupId":"CHK-008","requirement":"[G8.1] Material disadvantages, risk factors and limitations of the recommendation — Consistency: The suitability report must fairly, clearly, accurately and prominently explain all material disadvantages, risks, limitations, tax consequences, potential loss scenarios and consumer harms associated with the recommendations, and these explanations must be consistent with the supporting evidence provided.","reportSays":"The report discloses material disadvantages across three categories: (1) Material Disadvantages: \"The current fund is not in line with the agreed risk level and the plan offers only 6 funds\" (Nest); \"The existing scheme is a very simple auto-enrolment pension scheme, designed to provide a cheap pension for employers to meet pension legislation. It has few funds available and has to be managed by the member directly\" (Nest); \"The current portfolio is not in line with the agreed risk level\" (True Potential). (2) Limitations: \"All other risks associated with this investment are detailed in the product literature provided to you. You should read this document carefully\"; \"It is important to understand that the above charts are projections based on certain assumptions, and even a small variance between the assumptions used and actual experience can result in a marked difference in the outcome when compounded over a long period of time. Please also note that the chart does not consider any potential long-term care costs which could occur in later life\"; \"However, please be aware the cancellation notice includes a provision that any previous provider is not obliged to take the plan back.\" (3) Recommendation-specific disadvantages: Market timing risk (\"If the market rises in value between the date of realising your existing funds and the purchase of your new funds, you could be financially disadvantaged insofar as the cost of buying your new fund could be greater\"); Access delay (\"Should you require access to these funds in the future it will usually take 10-15 working days for administration, disinvestment and banking processes to complete\"); Slightly higher charges (\"Although the annual charges for the recommend plan are higher at 0.39% compared to 0.30%, this represents a relatively small increase of £19.15 per annum, based on the current values\"); Capital risk (\"Unit prices and the value of your pension fund can fall as well as rise and there is no guarantee you will receive a return of your original capital, especially in the early years or if you choose to take an income from it\"). The report does not explicitly quantify any loss scenario or show a projection where the recommended funds underperform materially.","fileSays":"The Aviva Pension Key Facts Document [P2] discloses multiple material risks: market timing risk (\"As a result of trading practices, there's a possibility that the price of investments may fall or rise in the period between us receiving your instructions and the time of the transaction\"); property investment delays (\"If the underlying investments include property, it may take some time before we can sell your investment\"); overseas investment currency risk (\"The value of any overseas investments will be influenced by the rate of exchange used to convert to sterling\"); emerging market risk (\"Investments in emerging or developing markets may carry higher risks as they may be subject to considerable fluctuations in value\"); small company risk (\"Small company investments may take longer to sell than those in larger companies, so the price fluctuations may be greater\"); drawdown depletion risk (\"Your investments need to grow to compensate for the income you withdraw. If that doesn't happen, the income you take will deplete your pension fund. Especially if you choose to take a high level of income\"); means-tested benefits impact (\"The income you take may have an impact on any means-tested benefits you're receiving\"). The Aviva Pension Terms & Conditions [P3, P7, P11] disclose restrictions on switching, trading, and access during certain conditions. The Aviva Pension KFD [P12] states: \"Transferring pensions isn't right for everyone. It could be a complex decision and you need to consider the charges, fund ranges, any valuable benefits that could be lost and tax implications\" and \"Remember that the value of your pension can go down as well as up and you may get back less than has been paid in. This may not be the case in your previous pension scheme.\" The Early Transfer Analysis documents [P9, P10, P14, P18] show projected fund values under different growth scenarios (0%, 2.94%, 5.88%) but do not explicitly show a loss scenario or underperformance relative to existing arrangements. The extraction notes identify that `/replacementAnalysis/0/featuresOrGuaranteesLost`, `/replacementAnalysis/1/featuresOrGuaranteesLost`, and `/replacementAnalysis/2/featuresOrGuaranteesLost` are absent from the report, meaning the report does not disclose what features or guarantees the client is giving up by transferring.","discrepancies":"[1] Features or guarantees lost: The report does not disclose what valuable benefits, features or guarantees the client is giving up by transferring from Nest (auto-enrolment scheme with limited funds) and True Potential (dormant pension) to the Aviva Platform. The Aviva KFD [P12] explicitly warns \"you may be giving up valuable rights in that scheme, some of these rights might be 'safeguarded benefits' and can't be replicated under the new scheme, so you need to be clear on what you may be giving up and be comfortable that the benefits of the transfer outweigh the loss of these benefits for you.\" The report does not address this. [2] Tax consequences of drawdown: The report does not explain the tax consequences of taking income in drawdown. The Aviva KFD [P2] warns that \"The income you take may have an impact on any means-tested benefits you're receiving\" and the Aviva T&C [P17] explains that income drawdown is available and that \"Each time You Crystallise We will normally pay You 25% of the value of the amount Crystallised as a Pension Commencement Lump Sum\" (tax-free) with the remainder allocated to provide income (taxable). The report does not explain these tax implications. [3] Pension inclusion in IHT from 6 April 2027: The report notes in the estate planning section that \"From 6 April 2027, most unused defined contribution pensions and death benefits (such as drawdown funds and uncrystallised pots) will be included in your taxable estate\" but does not quantify the impact or explain this as a material disadvantage of the recommended drawdown approach. The client's pension values (c. £327,945) will become part of the taxable estate, potentially triggering significant IHT liability when combined with the £350,000 inheritance. [4] Means-tested benefits impact: The report does not disclose the risk that drawdown income may affect means-tested benefits, which is disclosed in the Aviva KFD [P2]. [5] Loss scenario not illustrated: The report does not show a projection where the recommended funds underperform materially or where the client's retirement income falls short. The Early Transfer Analysis [P9, P10, P14, P18] shows three growth scenarios (0%, 2.94%, 5.88%) but the report does not discuss what happens if growth is negative or if the client's retirement income target is not met. [6] Cancellation and transfer restrictions: The report mentions cancellation rights [P4] but does not disclose the restrictions on transferring out (e.g., \"It's not possible to transfer structured products either into or out of your Pension Portfolio\" [P15]) or the requirement for full transfer of post-retirement accounts [P15]. [7] Charges comparison basis: The report states \"Although the annual charges for the recommend plan are higher at 0.39% compared to 0.30%, this represents a relatively small increase of £19.15 per annum, based on the current values.\" However, the extraction notes identify an internal inconsistency: the True Potential platform charge is stated as 0.40% in the existing arrangements table, but the replacement analysis section states platform charges of 0.40% plus ongoing advice charge of 0.50% plus weighted average fund management charges of 0.58%, totalling 1.48%. The comparison of 0.39% to 0.30% appears to compare only platform charges and does not include all ongoing charges, making the comparison incomplete and potentially misleading.","comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Quantified impact of pension inclusion in IHT from 6 April 2027 on the client's estate and tax liability","Explicit disclosure of what features, guarantees or safeguarded benefits are being given up by transferring from Nest and True Potential","Projection showing a loss scenario or material underperformance relative to existing arrangements","Detailed explanation of tax consequences of taking income in drawdown (25% tax-free lump sum, remainder taxable)","Disclosure of means-tested benefits impact of drawdown income","Clarification of the basis of the charge comparison (whether it includes all ongoing charges or only platform charges)"],"analysis":"The report discloses some material disadvantages and risks, but several material disadvantages, risks and tax consequences identified in the supporting evidence are absent or inadequately explained. (1) Features or guarantees lost: The extraction confirms that `/replacementAnalysis/0/featuresOrGuaranteesLost`, `/replacementAnalysis/1/featuresOrGuaranteesLost`, and `/replacementAnalysis/2/featuresOrGuaranteesLost` are not present in the report. The Aviva KFD [P12] explicitly warns that transferring pensions may involve giving up valuable rights or safeguarded benefits that cannot be replicated. The report identifies that Nest is a \"simple auto-enrolment pension scheme\" with \"few funds available\" but does not explain what specific benefits or protections the client is giving up. This is a material omission because the client is consolidating three separate pensions into one platform, and the report should disclose what is being lost in this process. (2) Tax consequences of drawdown: The report does not explain the tax treatment of drawdown income. The Aviva T&C [P17] explains that 25% of each crystallisation is paid as a tax-free lump sum and the remainder is allocated to provide income (which is taxable). The report does not disclose this, nor does it explain the interaction with the client's income tax position or means-tested benefits. (3) Pension inclusion in IHT from 6 April 2027: The report notes this change in the estate planning section but does not quantify the impact. The client's pension values (c. £327,945) will become part of the taxable estate from 6 April 2027. Combined with the £350,000 inheritance, this will take the client's total assets to c. £727,945 (excluding the main residence and other assets), which will trigger significant IHT liability. The report does not explain this as a material disadvantage of the recommended drawdown approach or discuss alternative strategies (e.g., accelerating drawdown before 6 April 2027, or using the pension to fund charitable gifts). (4) Means-tested benefits impact: The Aviva KFD [P2] warns \"The income you take may have an impact on any means-tested benefits you're receiving.\" The report does not disclose this risk, which is material because the client's retirement income target is £25,000-£30,000 per annum, and drawdown income could affect eligibility for benefits. (5) Loss scenario: The report does not illustrate a loss scenario or show what happens if the recommended funds underperform. The Early Transfer Analysis [P9, P10, P14, P18] shows three growth scenarios (0%, 2.94%, 5.88%) but the report does not discuss the 0% scenario or explain what the client's retirement income would be if growth is negative. This is a material omission because the report should show the client what could go wrong. (6) Charges comparison: The report states \"Although the annual charges for the recommend plan are higher at 0.39% compared to 0.30%, this represents a relatively small increase of £19.15 per annum, based on the current values.\" However, the extraction notes identify that the True Potential platform charge is stated as 0.40% in the existing arrangements table, but the replacement analysis section states total charges of 1.48% (0.40% platform + 0.50% advice + 0.58% fund charges). The comparison of 0.39% to 0.30% appears to compare only platform charges and does not include all ongoing charges. This makes the comparison incomplete and potentially misleading. The report should clarify what charges are included in each figure. (7) Cancellation and transfer restrictions: The report mentions cancellation rights but does not disclose the restrictions on transferring out or the requirement for full transfer of post-retirement accounts [P15]. (8) Vulnerability overlay: The client has been identified as having possible vulnerability due to recent divorce, employment change, and pending inheritance. The report does not explain how these vulnerabilities affect the suitability of the recommendation or whether additional safeguards are needed. For example, the pending inheritance is described as \"currently pending legal proceedings\" but the cashflow assumes receipt \"next year\" — the report does not explain the risk if the inheritance is delayed or does not materialise. The client's recent divorce and Pension Sharing Order may also mean the client is less familiar with pension planning, and the report should explain the risks more clearly. The report does disclose some material disadvantages (market timing risk, access delay, capital risk, projection limitations) and references product literature for additional risks. However, the omission of features/guarantees lost, tax consequences of drawdown, means-tested benefits impact, pension IHT inclusion impact, and loss scenarios represents a material gap in the disclosure of disadvantages and risks. The charge comparison is also potentially misleading because it does not clearly state what charges are included in each figure.","citations":[{"passageId":"P12","source":"Aviva Pension KFD.md","category":"H","quote":"you may be giving up valuable rights in that scheme, some of these rights might be 'safeguarded benefits' and can't be replicated under the new scheme, so you need to be clear on what you may be giving up and be comfortable that the benefits of the transfer outweigh the loss of these benefits for you.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Aviva Pension KFD.md","category":"H","quote":"Transferring pensions isn't right for everyone. It could be a complex decision and you need to consider the charges, fund ranges, any valuable benefits that could be lost and tax implications.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Aviva Pension KFD.md","category":"H","quote":"The income you take may have an impact on any means-tested benefits you're receiving. You may want to discuss this with your financial adviser.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Aviva Pension KFD.md","category":"H","quote":"Your investments need to grow to compensate for the income you withdraw. If that doesn't happen, the income you take will deplete your pension fund. Especially if you choose to take a high level of income.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P17","source":"Aviva Pension T&C.md","category":"H","quote":"Each time You Crystallise We will normally pay You 25% of the value of the amount Crystallised as a Pension Commencement Lump Sum, provided You have an entitlement to this. The remainder of Crystallised Benefit is then allocated to provide an income, although You have the option not to draw an income if You choose.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P15","source":"Aviva Pension KFD.md","category":"H","quote":"It's not possible to transfer structured products either into or ou