why leaving the funds in place was rejected. For Quilter, the evidence shows cost savings (0.08% = £208 per annum) and monitoring benefits, but does not explicitly reject the do-nothing option. For True Potential, the evidence shows cost savings (0.19% = £88.68 + £177.35 = £266.03 per annum) and risk alignment benefits, but again does not explicitly reject the do-nothing option. For Nest, the evidence shows a small cost increase (0.09% = £19.15 per annum) but emphasizes the benefit of professional management and wider fund range, yet does not explicitly reject the do-nothing option.\n\n**Guard application:**\nThe guard states: \"Options assessed once for the whole case may legitimately cover several plans — check whether the rejection reasoning is plan-specific where the plans differ materially.\" The three plans differ materially: Quilter is a Pension Sharing Order (new to the client), True Potential is a dormant personal pension with higher charges and misaligned risk, and Nest is a simple auto-enrolment scheme. The rejection reasoning provided (wider fund range, professional management) applies to all three but does not address their specific circumstances. For example, Nest's rejection does not address the fact that it is a paid-up scheme (no further contributions) and the client is now self-employed, whereas True Potential's rejection does not address the specific charge differential or risk misalignment.\n\n**Conclusion on comparison:**\nA comparison can be performed because the report makes explicit assertions about alternatives considered and rejection reasons. The discrepancies are between what the report asserts (alternatives considered, reasons provided) and what the evidence supports (limited evidence of consideration, generic rather than plan-specific rejection reasons, and no rejection reasons for the do-nothing alternative).\n\nThe requirement is not met because:\n1. The report asserts alternatives were considered but does not evidence consideration of all alternatives listed (particularly for Quilter: alternative product, employer scheme).\n2. The report provides rejection reasons for only some alternatives (in-situ switch for True Potential and Nest; none for do-nothing or other alternatives).\n3. The rejection reasons provided are not sufficiently plan-specific to demonstrate that the adviser considered the distinct circumstances of each plan.\n4. The report does not evidence why the do-nothing alternative was rejected for any plan, despite asserting it was considered.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"Whilst we could look at the Lower risk fund within this plan, the new scheme offers access to a wider range of investment options, allowing for a more tailored investment strategy that better suits your risk profile and retirement goals, and allowing greater choice should your risk profile change in the future","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"The receiving scheme has a lower overall cost structure compared to your existing pension, meaning more of your savings remain invested for growth rather than being lost to charges. The platform charges with Aviva are 0.21% compared to the True Potential platform charges of 0.40%, this represents a saving of 0.19% or £88.68 based on the current value of the fund.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"The existing scheme is a very simple auto-enrolment pension scheme, designed to provide a cheap pension for employers to meet pension legislation. It has few funds available and has to be managed by the member directly. You wish to take a more sophisticated approach and ensure that the pension is managed for you, which is why you have decided to take financial advice.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P9","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"Safeguarded Features incl. TFC/GARs/MIG: