er charge impact on the cost-benefit comparison. 5. No evidence that the Nest plan will become paid up following the client's employment change. 6. No projections or analysis addressing the client's stated intention to draw income from age 60, rather than the age 87 projections shown in the file. 7. No independent verification of the stated benefits (lower costs, ongoing monitoring, risk alignment, sophisticated management, transparency, flexible retirement options) against the switching costs.","analysis":"The requirement asks whether switching costs exceed the benefits. The report asserts they do not, presenting cost savings and non-financial benefits. However, a meaningful comparison cannot be performed because critical evidence is absent. For Quilter (79% of the transfer value), no switching analysis exists in the file — no charge comparison, no fund projections, no features analysis. The charge comparison table (P2) for True Potential and Nest excludes the new 1% ongoing adviser charge, which is the largest single recurring cost of the recommendation and must be included in any cost-benefit analysis. The report states an initial advice fee of £9,838.36 but provides no analysis of whether this upfront cost is justified by the benefits. The critical yield figures (3.37%, 0.41%, 6.32%) are stated but unsupported by projections in the file. The projections shown in the file (P1, P3, P4, P6, P7, P8) assume the funds are held to age 87, but the client's stated objective is to draw £25,000-£30,000 annually from age 60; this mismatch means the projections do not address the client's actual needs and therefore cannot support a cost-benefit analysis. The report claims \"flexible retirement options\" as a benefit of switching Nest, but the file records Nest as offering no flexi-access drawdown, undermining this claimed benefit. The report does not address whether the Nest plan will actually become paid up, which is a critical assumption in the switching rationale. Without