ess drawdown, full and partial UFPLS\" — the benefit claimed for switching away from Nest contradicts the existing position. [8] Quilter comparison: Quilter appears in neither the switching analysis tables nor the Pension Switch Charges table; no plan-specific comparison exists for 79% of the money being transferred.","comparisonPerformed":false,"missingInputs":["Quilter plan charges (platform, fund, advice)","Quilter fund holdings and performance data","Quilter safeguarded benefits or guarantees","Confirmation of True Potential existing fund holdings (report states £46,672.80 but only £41,055.26 of funds are listed)","Evidence that Nest plan will become paid up (depends on employment status change not evidenced)","Ongoing adviser charge (1% pa) impact on switching cost-benefit analysis","Comparison of retirement income projections under drawdown from age 60 (not age 87 paid-up)","Evidence of alternatives considered to switching","Basis for claim that recommended scheme offers 'wider range of investment options' than existing schemes","Documentation of safeguarded benefits or guarantees in existing arrangements that would be lost"],"analysis":"The requirement is to assess whether the report's switching recommendation is consistent with and supported by evidence of benefits and costs. The comparison cannot be performed reliably because:\n\n(1) **Quilter (79% of transfer value):** No Quilter documentation exists in the file. The report states platform charges of 0.27% but provides no corroborating evidence. The switching analysis for Quilter is absent from the SAP documents (P4-P14), which show only True Potential and Nest comparisons. This is the largest ceding plan by value and has no plan-specific cost/benefit analysis in the evidence.\n\n(2) **Charge comparison inconsistencies:** The report states three different charge differences for True Potential (0.21% vs 0.40%, then 0.09% difference, then 0.59% less expensive excluding advice charges). The Aviva platform cha